 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Two parents in Idaho mailed their five-year-old to Grandma.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.039
  },
  {
   "text": "The postage stamps went right on her coat.",
   "s": 3.339,
   "e": 5.013
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1914, parcel post took any package under 50 pounds.",
   "s": 5.313,
   "e": 8.803
  },
  {
   "text": "May weighed 48 and a half. So legally, she was a package.",
   "s": 9.103,
   "e": 11.957
  },
  {
   "text": "Mailing her cost less than a train ticket. So off she went.",
   "s": 12.257,
   "e": 14.8
  },
  {
   "text": "She rode in the mail car, between the sacks of letters.",
   "s": 15.1,
   "e": 17.435
  },
  {
   "text": "But she wasn't even the weirdest thing in the mail.",
   "s": 17.735,
   "e": 19.605
  },
  {
   "text": "In Utah, someone mailed a whole bank: 40 tons of bricks, 50 pounds at a time.",
   "s": 19.905,
   "e": 24.057
  },
  {
   "text": "So the Post Office banned mailing people.",
   "s": 24.357,
   "e": 26.116
  },
  {
   "text": "Too late. May was hand-delivered, right to Grandma.",
   "s": 26.416,
   "e": 28.628
  }
 ],
